# PHP test stubs / doubles

When a CLI test defines local PrestaShop doubles (`Configuration`, `PhpEncryption`, fake transports, etc.), give every parameter and return value an explicit PHP 8.1 type.

Intelephense analyzes these stubs statically. Untyped parameters produce editor noise such as `Parameter $key has no type information available`.

## Required pattern

```php
final class Configuration
{
    /** @var array<string, mixed> */
    public static $values = [];

    public static function updateValue(string $key, mixed $value): bool
    {
        self::$values[$key] = $value;

        return true;
    }

    public static function get(
        string $key,
        ?int $idLang = null,
        ?int $idShopGroup = null,
        ?int $idShop = null,
        mixed $default = false
    ): mixed {
        return self::$values[$key] ?? $default;
    }

    public static function deleteByName(string $key): bool
    {
        unset(self::$values[$key]);

        return true;
    }
}
```

## Rules

- Prefer native types (`string`, `int`, `bool`, `mixed`, nullable `?int`) over bare untyped params.
- Use `mixed` when the real PrestaShop API accepts heterogeneous values.
- Keep PHPDoc only when it adds array shape detail beyond `mixed`.
- Do not leave `public static function updateValue($key, $value)` style signatures in new or edited tests.
- Production code under `src/` and `unipayment.php` already follows typed style; this rule is specifically for test doubles that mimic core globals.
